#Gutenberg Hamlet

Word and speaker statistics for Shakespeare's Hamlet, drawn from the
Project Gutenberg plaintext edition (EBook #1524).  The analysis
machinery lives in #Gutenberg Corpus; this module applies it to
Hamlet and reports the results.

    _DATA  = Path(__file__).parent.parent / "data"
    HAMLET = load_play(_DATA / "pg1524.txt")

~example
    "HAMLET." in HAMLET
    len(HAMLET) > 100_000

##Word Frequencies

The top words in any English text are function words.  Finding "the"
at rank 1 and "and" at rank 2 confirms we have the right text in the
right language.

    WORDS = word_frequencies(HAMLET)

~example
    WORDS.most_common(1)[0][0] == "the"
    WORDS.most_common(2)[1][0] == "and"

##Speaking Parts

Hamlet has roughly three times as many turns as the next character.
That dominance is not incidental; it is what the play is built around.

    SPEAKERS = parse_speakers(HAMLET)
    _TOP     = SPEAKERS.most_common(10)

~example
    SPEAKERS.most_common(1)[0][0] == "HAMLET"
    SPEAKERS["HAMLET"] > SPEAKERS["KING"] * 2

The report function summarises all three measures.  It is defined
here so it can be tested independently; the ~run claim below invokes
it when the module is executed.

    def report() -> None:
        print(f"{'=' * 44}")
        print(f"  Hamlet — text statistics")
        print(f"{'=' * 44}")
        print(f"  Words  : {sum(WORDS.values()):>8,}  total")
        print(f"           {len(WORDS):>8,}  unique")
        print()
        print(f"  Top 10 words:")
        for _w, _n in WORDS.most_common(10):
            print(f"    {_w:<16} {_n:>5,}")
        print()
        print(f"  Top 10 speakers (turns):")
        for _s, _n in _TOP:
            print(f"    {_s:<20} {_n:>4,}")
        print(f"{'=' * 44}")

~run
    report()

---

#Tests

##text loading
    len(HAMLET) > 100_000
    HAMLET.startswith("\n") or "THE TRAGEDY" in HAMLET[:500]

##word frequencies
    WORDS["the"] > 500
    WORDS["the"] > WORDS["hamlet"]
    WORDS.most_common(1)[0][0] == "the"
    sum(WORDS.values()) > 20_000

##speakers
    SPEAKERS["HAMLET"] > 300
    SPEAKERS["HAMLET"] > SPEAKERS["KING"]

This is the headline fact of the analysis — the character the play is
named after is also the one who speaks the most, by turn count.

~test hamlet_is_most_frequent_speaker
    SPEAKERS.most_common(1)[0][0] == "HAMLET"

